th='Adventure, curiosity and an unlikely friendship; respect for wildlife – the watchman protects both the train and the leopard.',
sum='Suraj, an adventure-loving boy, cycles out of town to a railway cutting in the jungle to watch the midday steam engine roar out of a tunnel like a green, black and gold dragon. He walks through the dark, smoky tunnel and, coming out, glimpses a flash of orange and gold on the hillside. Sunder Singh, the tunnel watchman, welcomes him to his hut – he calls it “my tunnel, lent to the Government” and says the animal was “my leopard”, which minds its own business. His duty is to walk the tunnel every day and night; if there is danger he waves a red flag (by day) or his lamp (by night) to warn the engine driver. Suraj returns the next evening to see the night mail. While they wait, they hear a sawing sound – the leopard is in the tunnel. To save it from being run over, Sunder Singh takes his lamp and axe, Suraj follows, and together they shout until the leopard runs away. The train thunders past. A week later Suraj travels in the same night mail with his father to Delhi; near the tunnel he sees the watchman’s lamp and thinks of Sunder Singh as a firefly who lights up the darkness for steam engines and leopards.',
